// DLR ADC/BT — bare acquisition test.
//
//   timer ..... paces the pipeline; the DLR block takes ONE frame per tick
//   dlrAdc .... opens the serial port, streams, decodes frames into volts
//
// BEFORE RUNNING:
//
//   1. Pair the board and find its OUTGOING Bluetooth COM port
//      (Settings > Bluetooth & devices > More Bluetooth settings > COM Ports tab).
//      Put that port in Params[0] below, or pick it on the block's card.
//
//   2. Set "DesiredRate" on the timer to the board's ACTUAL frame rate. This is the
//      one setting that matters. Each tick consumes exactly one frame, so:
//        timer too slow -> buffer overflows, oldest frames dropped, error count climbs
//        timer too fast -> ticks find nothing, the last sample is republished (stair steps)
//      The raw-mode harness prints the measured board rate; use that number.
//
//   3. Params[1] must match how many channels the board is actually configured for.
//      The block sends this count to the board at Connect, and sizes its frames from it:
//      frame = 4 + 2*channels + 4 bytes. A mismatch shows up as "Length mismatch" errors.
//
// The block does NOT auto-connect. Load the pipeline, then press Connect on the card.
//
// PARAMS: [ portName, numChannels, baudRate ]
//   portName ..... "COM7", or a bare number like 7 (both resolve to COM7). Empty = pick on the card.
//   numChannels .. ADC channels to request, 1..255. Default 10.
//   baudRate ..... default 115200; the board ships at this.
//
// Adding "Path" to a block writes every published sample to a timestamped CSV in that
// directory — the way to check the recording afterwards rather than watching the scope.
{
  "timer": {
    "Type": "ClockBlock",
    "DesiredRate": 100
  },

  "dlrAdc": {
    "Type": "DlrAdcBt",
    "Inputs": ["timer"],
    "Params": ["COM7", 10, 115200]
    // "Path": "C:/Data/dlr_session1"
  }
}
